---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import ClosingBand from "../components/ClosingBand.astro";
import FramedPhoto from "../components/FramedPhoto.astro";
import photos from "../data/photos.json";
import imgRepair from "../assets/photos/tech-repair.jpg";

const machineSpecs = [
  { part: "Compute", detail: "Single-board computer, no accelerator, no cloud call" },
  { part: "Vision", detail: "Camera module in a fixed, controlled-light classification chamber" },
  { part: "Weight", detail: "Load cell for weight verification" },
  { part: "Insertion", detail: "Infrared sensor for insertion detection" },
  { part: "Gate", detail: "Servo-actuated — accepts or rejects" },
  { part: "Cabinet", detail: "Steel, fabricated locally" },
];

const repairPoints = [
  "Locally fabricated cabinet",
  "Commodity compute",
  "Standard connectors",
  "Parts obtainable in Lagos or Ibadan",
];
---

<Base
  title="Technology — Green Africa"
  description="Onboard computer vision, offline-first records and Hedera consensus logging, built for African operating conditions."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Technology"
    headline="Designed around the power and the network we actually have."
    subhead="Intermittent power, patchy connectivity, and import costs that make European hardware a non-starter. Every technical decision here starts from those facts."
  />

  <!-- The machine -->
  <section class="rule-t">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf">01 — The machine</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          The chamber does most of the work.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <p>
          Fixing the camera, controlling the lighting and taking one bottle at a time turns a hard
          computer vision problem, object detection in an uncontrolled scene, into a much easier
          one: single-object classification under known conditions. That is what lets a small
          quantized model run accurately on inexpensive hardware, without an accelerator and without
          a cloud call.
        </p>
        <p>
          Machines that accept a whole bag of mixed material need much more compute, mechanism and
          maintenance, and produce a less trustworthy count for it.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-2 border border-ink/15">
          <p class="border-b border-ink/15 bg-sand/60 px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-ink/60">
            Inside a station
          </p>
          <dl>
            {
              machineSpecs.map((spec) => (
                <div class="grid gap-1 border-b border-ink/10 px-4 py-3 last:border-b-0 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-4">
                  <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-ink/50">
                    {spec.part}
                  </dt>
                  <dd class="text-sm text-ink/75 sm:col-span-2">{spec.detail}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Offline-first -->
  <section class="rule-t">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf">02 — Offline-first by default</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          Designed to work with no network at all.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <p>
          Records are signed on the device the moment a deposit is verified, then written to a
          local hash-chained store. Each record includes the hash of the one before it, so any
          later alteration breaks the chain visibly. When connectivity returns, the queue submits
          to the ledger in order.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice the machine goes on paying and counting through an outage without losing its
          evidential integrity. Nothing is lost, and nothing can be quietly rewritten in the gap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why a public ledger -->
  <section class="rule-t">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf">03 — Why a public ledger</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          A filing cabinet nobody can break into.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <p>
          We record deposits on Hedera. The reason is narrow and specific: we want the recovery
          record to be checkable by people who have no reason to trust us.
        </p>
        <p>
          A database we control is only ever as credible as our reputation. A public consensus
          record is checkable independently, with timestamps we can't backdate and entries we can't
          delete. For a claim that will end up in a compliance filing or an assurance process, that
          difference is what makes the record usable.
        </p>
        <p>
          We use Hedera specifically for transaction costs low enough to justify writing a record
          for a single bottle, and finality fast enough to matter operationally.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are not issuing a token to the public, and there is nothing here to speculate on. We
          use the ledger as a filing cabinet, nothing more.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Built to be fixed -->
  <section class="bg-pine text-paper">
    <div class="container-x grid gap-10 py-16 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-12">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow text-leaf-bright">04 — Built to be fixed</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance">
          Still running in year three.
        </h2>
        <FramedPhoto
          src={imgRepair}
          alt={photos["tech-repair"].alt}
          credit={`Photo: ${photos["tech-repair"].photographer} / Pexels`}
          dark
          class="mt-8"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/75 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7" data-reveal style="--d:120ms">
        <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2.5">
          {
            repairPoints.map((point) => (
              <li class="border border-mint/25 px-3.5 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-mint">
                {point}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          A machine that can only be repaired by flying in a technician is a demonstration, not
          infrastructure. Everything above is chosen so a technician in Lagos or Ibadan can keep it
          running.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ClosingBand
    headline="Technical due diligence welcome."
    body="If you want to look at the record structure, query the ledger yourself, or pull apart the verification logic, we'll walk you through it."
  />
</Base>
